  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nUpdate: We continue to recover this afternoon with MLCapes now up to\naround 4000 J/KG across much of our west. There is still an \ninstability min across central Ohio due to the earlier convection, \nbut there is some concern that even this area may be able to recover \nto some degree. A remnant boundary from the earlier convection may \nserve as a focus for additional convection as we progress through \nthe rest of the afternoon. If storms do get going, some severe \nstorms will be possible given the degree of instability. Damaging \nwind and possibly large hail will be the main threats. Severe\nThunderstorm Watch #554 has been expanded to include the rest of our\narea (in effect until 8 PM). \n\nPrevious discussion:\nThunderstorm complex moving southeast across south central Ohio this\nafternoon and is just about out of our area. It has pushed out a \nboundary that is currently moving slowly southwest across southwest \nOhio. This is moving into a moderately unstable airmass where MLCapes\nare in the 3000-3500 J/KG range. We will have to watch this boundary\nfor some additional redevelopment as we progress through the rest of\nthe afternoon. \n\nIn west to northwest flow aloft, several rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms look likely through mid week but timing and location \nremain uncertain. There is an increase in the low level jet late \ntonight into Tuesday morning so some additional redevelopment may be \npossible then. As we destabilize through the day, expect a stronger \nline of thunderstorms to affect the area Tuesday afternoon into \nTuesday evening as a mid level short wave moves through. This will be\naccompanied by seasonably impressive deep layer shear in the 40 to \n60 knot range. As a result, expect the potential for severe storms \nwith Tuesday afternoon into the evening. Damaging wind will be the \nprimary threat. \n\nIt looks like another mid level short wave will move through the \narea Wednesday into Wednesday night, leading to another round of \nstorms. Deep layer shear does not look quite as strong as Tuesday, \nbut some severe storms will remain possible, with damaging wind still\nthe main threat.\n\nWith high PWs in place, locally heavy rainfall will be possible with\nthe storms. While there remains some uncertainty with thee location \nof the storms, wanted to get an FFA out as repeated rounds over the \nsame locations will result in an increased Flash Flood risk. This may\nneed to be expanded in both space and time over the next couple o\nshifts, depending on convective trends.",
